THEN ATTACK THE LOGIC

For every major part of the project ask:

“Does this actually make sense?”

Not:

“Is this coded correctly?”

I care about the reasoning behind the project.

Find anything where:

* Input does not logically lead to output
* Output does not lead to a useful action
* A metric doesn’t actually mean what we claim it means
* A score has no defensible interpretation
* A feature exists only because it sounds cool
* AI is being used where rules would be better
* AI isn’t being used where it actually would provide value
* The problem is artificially created
* The solution doesn’t solve the stated problem
* The proposed solution creates a new problem
* Assumptions are unrealistic
* The architecture doesn’t match the real-world scenario
* The threat model doesn’t make sense
* The attacker model doesn’t make sense
* The defender model doesn’t make sense
* The user workflow doesn’t make sense
* The business model doesn’t make sense
* The FinTech connection feels forced
* The smart-city connection feels forced
* Two features contradict each other
* One feature makes another feature unnecessary
* The system measures the wrong thing
* The system optimizes the wrong thing
* The system detects something but cannot explain what to do
* The system gives an alert without meaningful action
* The system prioritizes the wrong risk
* The system confuses anomaly with attack
* The system confuses trust with security
* The system confuses risk with threat
* The system confuses technical severity with financial impact
* The system produces numbers that look scientific but have no real justification

